WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:04.170
This lesson will dive deeper into how core
memory is designed and implemented.

2
00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:05.872
We'll also go through an example

3
00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:09.509
of how you can customize core memory
with custom memory blocks and tools.

4
00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:11.511
Let's get to it.

5
00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.980
Core memory is defined by memory blocks,

6
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:17.484
which are basically subsections of memory
and memory tools.

7
00:00:17.951 --> 00:00:22.088
The core memory within the context window
is actually divided into multiple blocks,

8
00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:25.158
where each block corresponds
to some kind of character limit,

9
00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.995
which defines how much of the context
window can be used up by that block.

10
00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:30.630
The block also has a label

11
00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.134
such as human or persona,
which can be used to reference the block.

12
00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.903
And finally,
of course, the block has a value,

13
00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.973
so this is the actual data
that's placed into the context window.

14
00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:42.575
So for example "My name is Sarah."

15
00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:46.112
In addition to these blocks,
core memory also has tools

16
00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:47.714
associated with that memory.

17
00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:52.252
So for example you might have core memory
replace where specifies the block label

18
00:00:52.252 --> 00:00:55.488
in this case human,
as well as the old content Sarah,

19
00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.590
which is getting replaced
by the new content,

20
00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.960
Bob. Block data is compiled
in the context window

21
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.097
at inference time
to make up the core memory context.

22
00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:08.435
So for example, for the human block,
you might have the tag

23
00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:10.236
that shows the section human.

24
00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:13.506
And then this tag will also include
the number of characters

25
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:17.243
out of the total budget that's being used
so far, in addition to the value.

26
00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.780
Memory blocks are synced to a database
and have unique IDs,

27
00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.783
so they can actually be shared
across multiple agents

28
00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:27.754
by syncing the block value
to multiple agents context window.

29
00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.658
So to start, let's first once again
initialize

30
00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:34.661
our Letta client and connect it
to our locally running server.

31
00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:38.465
We're also going to once again
paste in this custom

32
00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:41.434
helper function
for printing out Letta messages.

33
00:01:41.434 --> 00:01:44.504
So we actually modified it this time
because this lesson

34
00:01:44.504 --> 00:01:48.575
we're actually going to use streaming
responses since we'll have more complex

35
00:01:48.575 --> 00:01:51.578
agent interactions
which will be many steps long.

36
00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.313
So we basically want the new step

37
00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:56.316
stream back to us as opposed
to waiting for them to all complete.

38
00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:00.420
So in the streaming scenario,
the user should his six actually also

39
00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:04.891
get returned as a special message type
that has a message type usage statistics.

40
00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:08.361
So we'll also print these out
and then also have a big line

41
00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:11.464
between different steps so that we can
kind of read them more easily.

42
00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.567
So, like we went over last time,

43
00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.537
core memory consists
of multiple different memory blocks,

44
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.940
and each block represents
a section of the LLMs context window

45
00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:24.177
that's reserved to storing memories
related to that block.

46
00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:28.581
We can create an agent
that has a human and persona block,

47
00:02:29.082 --> 00:02:32.852
and then also list out
the blocks that are created by this agent.

48
00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:36.422
So we can do this by calling the client's
block list function

49
00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.626
and specifying the agent ID.

50
00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.662
So we can see that
this returns list of block objects

51
00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:46.799
that have the value of the human block,
and then also the value of the persona

52
00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.301
block. And we can also see things

53
00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:50.970
like the character limit
that's assigned to this block.

54
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:54.274
The agent is not allowed
to make this memory block

55
00:02:54.274 --> 00:02:56.543
longer than this number of characters.

56
00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:59.279
So we can make sure that the context
window doesn't overflow

57
00:02:59.279 --> 00:03:02.916
or become overly focused on
just the in-context memory blocks.

58
00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.084
And then,
like we mentioned in the last lesson,

59
00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:08.521
there's also these unique IDs
which we can use to access the blocks

60
00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.589
directly from the client.

61
00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:14.127
So let's go for an example of directly
accessing the blocks.

62
00:03:14.294 --> 00:03:17.530
We can store
this block ID by copy-pasting

63
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.533
one of these block ID strings.

64
00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:22.535
And now that we have

65
00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.872
this block ID saved,
we can actually also query the client,

66
00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.942
with this block ID to get back
this block as well.

67
00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:32.378
So if you want to modify this block
or get this block, you can do that

68
00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.381
by passing the block ID.

69
00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.850
You can also access block by specifying

70
00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.954
a combination of the agent ID
and then also the block label.

71
00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:43.590
So the block label will always be unique

72
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.757
for specific agents.

73
00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:47.961
So you can't have multiple block labels
that have the label human.

74
00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:52.198
Blocks are represented
inside of the context window,

75
00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:55.702
and a special section of the context
window dedicated to core memory.

76
00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:59.005
So we can actually also see,
for a specific agent,

77
00:03:59.205 --> 00:04:01.941
what the template is for
how the blocks are shown.

78
00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:05.945
So this is actually the prompt template
for how the blocks are represented.

79
00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.182
We can see that it has a tag
that has the block label.

80
00:04:09.449 --> 00:04:12.385
And then it also communicates,
through this,

81
00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:15.722
how much of the total length
is being used of the limit.

82
00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:19.158
And then of course, inside of the tag,
it also contains the block value.

83
00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:21.995
So you can actually customize this
if you want to change

84
00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:24.731
the way that the blocks are rendered
instead of the context window.

85
00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:28.334
So like we went over in the last lesson,

86
00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:32.105
Letta agents have access to tools
like core memory replace and core

87
00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:35.642
memory append
to actually edit their in-context memory.

88
00:04:36.009 --> 00:04:38.411
And the way that this implementation
actually works

89
00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:42.248
is that we're able to define tools
that we attach to the agent,

90
00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:46.519
that are able to modify the agents
internal state or blocks.

91
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.823
Before we get into how to customize
in context memory management,

92
00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:54.927
we're first going to quickly go over,
how you can define stateful tools,

93
00:04:54.927 --> 00:04:56.195
inside of Letta, are tools

94
00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.432
that are able to modify
the actual agent state through the tool.

95
00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.903
So, a really simple example of this is
this tool here,

96
00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:05.838
which is getting the agent ID.

97
00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:09.642
So we pass in a special argument
into the tool definition.

98
00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:12.612
So the field that has the type
agent state.

99
00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.615
And we'll put this in quotes
just so that we don't get import errors.

100
00:05:16.282 --> 00:05:20.286
All this tool does is get the idea
of the agent by accessing the agent state,

101
00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:24.324
So we can create this tool
by calling upsert_from_function.

102
00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:26.326
You can also do create from function.

103
00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:28.494
There's a couple different ways
to create tools with Letta

104
00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.930
but this is perhaps the simplest.

105
00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:31.898
You'll just need to make sure

106
00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:35.601
that your docstring contains
all the fields, except you don't need to

107
00:05:35.668 --> 00:05:39.572
specify any documentation for agent state,
since the Letta system will know

108
00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:42.909
to inject the agent state of the agent
when this tool is called.

109
00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:46.112
So we'll create this get id tool.

110
00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.247
So now that we've created

111
00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:51.250
this tool, we can create an agent
that's actually using the tool.

112
00:05:51.317 --> 00:05:52.952
So for this agent,
we'll make it really simple.

113
00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:54.854
It'll have no in-context memory.

114
00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:58.224
And we'll basically attach an extra tool,
this get ID tool,

115
00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:01.227
by passing in the ID into tool IDs.

116
00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:03.730
And then we can basically message this

117
00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:06.733
agent to ask you what is your agent ID.

118
00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:07.533
One thing to note

119
00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.170
is that we're actually now calling
create stream instead of just create here.

120
00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:15.908
This basically is telling Letta we want
to get back the responses as a stream,

121
00:06:16.109 --> 00:06:18.244
as opposed
to just the entirety of the response,

122
00:06:18.244 --> 00:06:21.247
which is what we were doing
in the last lesson.

123
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.217
So we can see that
we're now getting back the responses.

124
00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:28.154
So first, the agent reasons, that,
you know,

125
00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:32.058
the user wants to know the agent ID,
and so it calls this get agent ID tool.

126
00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:35.428
It gets back this response,
which is its own agent ID,

127
00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:39.866
and then it sends back a message to
the user telling us what its agent ID was.

128
00:06:40.199 --> 00:06:43.102
And you can see at the end,
that we also print out this usage

129
00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:46.739
statistics message, because we are getting
a streaming response that gives us

130
00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:50.309
the usage information like the token usage
and also the step count.

131
00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.680
So now we've gone over
our given an example of how to access

132
00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.015
agent state through tools.

133
00:06:56.015 --> 00:07:00.052
And so now we can try defining
a more customized form of in-context

134
00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:03.189
memory management by customizing
the memory management format.

135
00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:04.157
And also tools.

136
00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:05.691
In this

137
00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:08.795
last section we're going to create
a custom task queue memory.

138
00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:10.229
So this is going to look a little

139
00:07:10.229 --> 00:07:14.066
bit different than the memory that we had
before where we had a human and persona

140
00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:18.471
that were edited by the default core
memory replace and core memory append tools.

141
00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:22.375
So we're going to create
custom memory management tools,

142
00:07:22.375 --> 00:07:26.412
and then also kind of customize the format
of the actual in-context memory.

143
00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.751
So the first memory management tool
we're going to define is task queue push.

144
00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:35.588
So, instead of having human and persona
sections we're instead going to have

145
00:07:35.888 --> 00:07:37.924
the in-context memory be a list.

146
00:07:37.924 --> 00:07:41.461
So this is going to take
in the special agent state, argument,

147
00:07:41.461 --> 00:07:44.263
and then also a task description string.

148
00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:47.066
And in order to make sure
this tool gets created properly,

149
00:07:47.066 --> 00:07:49.335
we have to provide a properly formatted
docstring.

150
00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:51.137
So this will have the description.

151
00:07:51.137 --> 00:07:51.971
Push to a task

152
00:07:51.971 --> 00:07:55.208
queue sort into our memory and then we also
have to define the arguments.

153
00:07:55.208 --> 00:07:58.778
So like I mentioned before, we don't need
to actually define agent state.

154
00:07:58.778 --> 00:08:00.746
The system will we'll know to pass

155
00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:03.716
that in. The LLM
doesn't need to know about agent State.

156
00:08:03.783 --> 00:08:07.620
And so we just tell the LLM
in this docstring about task description,

157
00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.790
which is a description of the next task
to accomplish.

158
00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:15.027
And then, the return is just null,
because we won't return back a

159
00:08:15.027 --> 00:08:16.429
response for this function.

160
00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:18.264
And so

161
00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:21.334
what actually
happens for the execution of task queue push

162
00:08:21.634 --> 00:08:25.171
is that we're going to actually add
a new task into the core memory.

163
00:08:25.438 --> 00:08:27.406
We're going to represent as a list.

164
00:08:27.406 --> 00:08:29.609
And the way we could do this
is that we can actually connect

165
00:08:29.609 --> 00:08:32.612
to the Letta client
from inside of this tool.

166
00:08:32.712 --> 00:08:35.114
And so what we can do is
we can do all the same things

167
00:08:35.114 --> 00:08:38.117
that we would do with the client
from inside of this tool.

168
00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.253
So it's a little confusing.

169
00:08:40.253 --> 00:08:43.589
The agent can also itself
access the client through tools.

170
00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:47.927
So by creating this client
we can then retrieve

171
00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:51.564
the current in-context memory block
that has the block label tasks.

172
00:08:51.564 --> 00:08:54.200
So what we'll create the agent
that has this later.

173
00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.869
And we can basically load in the tasks.

174
00:08:56.869 --> 00:09:01.207
Because this block that has the label
tasks will also be like a

175
00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:03.209
JSON string list.

176
00:09:03.209 --> 00:09:06.212
And then append the new task into tasks.

177
00:09:06.379 --> 00:09:09.382
And then save back
this new list of tasks.

178
00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:13.786
By now calling blocks modify.
And we have access to the agent state

179
00:09:13.786 --> 00:09:16.389
so we can of course,
access the ID like we did before

180
00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:20.026
and the other tool to basically modify,
this agent in-context

181
00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:20.426
memory.

182
00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:23.763
So that will be the first one.

183
00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:26.732
So, the other function
we're going to define is task queue pop.

184
00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.435
So this also has access
to the agent state.

185
00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:31.871
All it does is pop out
the next task from the task queue.

186
00:09:31.871 --> 00:09:33.940
So basically removing that from the queue.

187
00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:37.076
And then what it's going to return is
the remaining tasks inside of the queue.

188
00:09:37.643 --> 00:09:41.314
So very similarly we're again
going to instantiate an instance of the

189
00:09:41.314 --> 00:09:46.314
Letta client in this tool, get the task,
task block from the in-context memory.

190
00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:50.356
And then we're going to load in the task
by loading the JSON string.

191
00:09:50.556 --> 00:09:52.892
If there's nothing, inside of the task

192
00:09:52.892 --> 00:09:54.427
then we just return none.

193
00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:57.430
And then otherwise we take
the first task into the queue.

194
00:09:57.563 --> 00:10:01.367
And so this kind of grabs that first task,
and then we also then update

195
00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:02.435
the remaining tasks.

196
00:10:02.435 --> 00:10:05.805
So now we just remove the first task
from this queue,

197
00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:08.975
and get the JSON string from it,

198
00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:13.975
and then write that as the new value
for the in-context memory block for tasks.

199
00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:17.483
And then finally we'll again
return these remaining tasks.

200
00:10:18.651 --> 00:10:19.819
So now that we've defined

201
00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:23.489
both of these functions, we can upsert
both of these as tools into Letta.

202
00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:27.627
So we'll create the task queue pop tool
and then also the task queue push tool.

203
00:10:28.227 --> 00:10:31.130
Tools that can be called inside of
Letta agents.

204
00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:34.066
So now that we've created these tools,
we can actually create an agent,

205
00:10:34.066 --> 00:10:35.668
that has these tools attached.

206
00:10:35.668 --> 00:10:38.104
So we're going to create something
called a task agent,

207
00:10:38.104 --> 00:10:41.507
which only has access to the task
queue pop and task

208
00:10:41.507 --> 00:10:43.743
queue push memory management tools.

209
00:10:43.743 --> 00:10:47.313
So we're also going to actually override
the default system prompt.

210
00:10:47.313 --> 00:10:50.516
Since we did change the way
that memory is managed, we have a custom

211
00:10:50.516 --> 00:10:53.519
system prompt that kind of describes
how that's done to the agent.

212
00:10:53.519 --> 00:10:55.788
So you can read this if you're interested.

213
00:10:55.788 --> 00:10:58.724
And then for the memory blocks,
instead of having human and persona

214
00:10:58.724 --> 00:11:01.727
sections we're instead
just going to have a tasks block.

215
00:11:01.727 --> 00:11:02.728
And we're going to initialize

216
00:11:02.728 --> 00:11:06.098
that with a value which is just a JSON
dumps of an empty list.

217
00:11:06.565 --> 00:11:07.500
And so this is a list

218
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.770
that we're going to add to
or remove things from with our tools.

219
00:11:11.303 --> 00:11:14.640
And then we define the LLM model
and the embedding model.

220
00:11:15.107 --> 00:11:17.009
Attach these tool IDs.

221
00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:20.112
And then we're actually also going
to disable the default tools.

222
00:11:20.112 --> 00:11:23.182
So by passing in false for include base
tools.

223
00:11:23.582 --> 00:11:25.584
We're actually not going to add
all the default tools

224
00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:29.021
like the core memory or archival memory
and conversation search tools.

225
00:11:29.388 --> 00:11:32.291
We're
only going to have the task queue-related tools,

226
00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:34.560
but we will also add
the send message tool.

227
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:36.495
So we do want this included.

228
00:11:36.495 --> 00:11:40.666
Just because otherwise the agent won't be able
to return back assistant messages.

229
00:11:42.368 --> 00:11:45.571
So now we've created the agent and
we can actually see, what tools it has.

230
00:11:45.571 --> 00:11:48.808
this agent only has access
to these three tools.

231
00:11:49.842 --> 00:11:52.411
And then we can also see
what's inside of its memory.

232
00:11:52.411 --> 00:11:55.781
So basically reading
the value of its current, task block

233
00:11:56.615 --> 00:11:59.285
and we can see it's just an empty list.

234
00:11:59.285 --> 00:12:01.454
So now we can actually message the agent,

235
00:12:01.454 --> 00:12:04.957
to start adding
some tasks into its task memory.

236
00:12:04.957 --> 00:12:08.427
So we can create a streaming message,
to this task agent

237
00:12:08.728 --> 00:12:10.629
and tell it in two separate tasks,

238
00:12:10.629 --> 00:12:12.331
the first start calling me Charles,

239
00:12:12.331 --> 00:12:16.001
and then tell me a haiku about my name
and add these as two different tasks.

240
00:12:16.969 --> 00:12:20.106
So we can see that we start
getting the responses stream back.

241
00:12:20.106 --> 00:12:23.809
And so, it reasons
that the user wants to create two tasks.

242
00:12:24.176 --> 00:12:25.010
It calls task

243
00:12:25.010 --> 00:12:28.114
queue push on the first task,
which is start calling me Charles.

244
00:12:29.081 --> 00:12:30.916
Then it calls task queue push

245
00:12:30.916 --> 00:12:33.919
on the second task,
tell me a haiku about my name.

246
00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:38.591
And then it realizes that now it has tasks
that it needs to accomplish.

247
00:12:38.591 --> 00:12:41.927
So, it's actually told in its instructions
that it should be clearing its task

248
00:12:41.927 --> 00:12:45.297
queue if there are tasks in there.
So then it starts to go back

249
00:12:45.297 --> 00:12:49.335
and pop items from its task queue.
So, it pops the first task.

250
00:12:49.335 --> 00:12:52.271
And so now we just have one remaining
task in the queue,

251
00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:56.075
and then it pops it again
until the remaining tasks is empty.

252
00:12:56.175 --> 00:12:59.979
And so once it's finally cleared
all its tasks, it, returns back a message.

253
00:12:59.979 --> 00:13:00.646
Which is "got it.

254
00:13:00.646 --> 00:13:02.181
Charles. From now on, I'll call you that."

255
00:13:02.181 --> 00:13:03.783
And then here's a haiku about your name.

256
00:13:03.783 --> 00:13:05.918
And so it returns back Haiku.

257
00:13:05.918 --> 00:13:07.787
"Charles. Strong and wise.

258
00:13:07.787 --> 00:13:09.421
Gentle winds whisper your name.

259
00:13:09.421 --> 00:13:11.624
Stars light up the night."

260
00:13:11.624 --> 00:13:14.527
So there's quite a lot done
in this sequence of events.

261
00:13:14.527 --> 00:13:18.798
we can actually see we had a step count
of five, and a lot of tokens used up,

262
00:13:19.298 --> 00:13:22.902
which is why it's often useful to have
streaming for these more complex tasks,

263
00:13:22.902 --> 00:13:25.638
because we can actually see
the progress of the agent over time.

264
00:13:26.739 --> 00:13:29.008
So the LLM is a little probabilistic.

265
00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:32.511
So you might have seen your agent
not actually complete all of its tasks,

266
00:13:32.511 --> 00:13:36.348
in which case you can once again
message it to complete your tasks.

267
00:13:37.216 --> 00:13:40.319
So you might need to do that
if your task queue still has items in it.

268
00:13:40.386 --> 00:13:41.921
In our case, there was nothing left.

269
00:13:41.921 --> 00:13:45.624
The agent did actually get everything done
so you can see it return back

270
00:13:45.624 --> 00:13:48.627
all tasks are complete
so didn't actually run anything.

271
00:13:49.195 --> 00:13:52.665
And finally, we can also retrieve
the tasks to see if it's empty.

272
00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:55.234
And the agent
has run through all of its tasks.

273
00:13:55.234 --> 00:13:58.737
So we've now implemented a custom
form of in-context memory

274
00:13:58.737 --> 00:14:03.737
by customizing the memory blocks and also,
writing pretty advanced stateful tools

275
00:14:04.643 --> 00:14:05.878
that can actually modify

276
00:14:05.878 --> 00:14:10.115
the memory of the agent and also access
a Letta client from inside of the tool.

277
00:14:10.416 --> 00:14:13.919
This is actually a really powerful
framework for building stateful agents.

278
00:14:14.286 --> 00:14:17.423
So you can create very advanced examples
that really control

279
00:14:17.423 --> 00:14:21.160
the way that in-context memory
is managed and modified over time.

280
00:14:21.427 --> 00:14:25.030
Hopefully this simple tasks example
gives you inspiration for how you could

281
00:14:25.364 --> 00:14:28.601
create advanced ways of managing memory
for your own applications.

